title: 'Docker: stats(stream => 1) on a stopped container streams zero readings forever'
updated: 2026-08-27T18:34:37Z
---

Measured 2026-08-27 on Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55) while cross-checking #45.

GET /containers/{id}/stats?stream=true on a container that is NOT running
answers 200 chunked and then emits one zero-filled stats object per second,
forever. Measured: 5 objects in 5s, 12 objects in 12s, curl exit 28 both
times. No container exit will ever terminate it, because the container has
already exited.

Podman does the opposite: one error object, connection closed after 1.5ms.

So the two engines fail in opposite directions on the same call, and the
"bound the window" advice this distribution gives for streaming endpoints
does not apply here -- there is no window to bound. Only on_event plus a
$stop->() call gets out.

Second half of the same measurement, and the reason this is not just #45's
Docker column: Docker's ONE-SHOT answer (stream=false) on a stopped
container is a real, structurally valid stats object -- id, name, os_type,
cpu_stats, precpu_stats, blkio_stats all present and zero-filled,
Content-Length: 829. It is not distinguishable by shape from a live
reading. The only markers are the Go zero time in `read`
("0001-01-01T00:00:00Z") and num_procs: 0.

That invalidates the advice currently in the stats POD, which tells the
caller to "test for read or cpu_stats before using what comes back". On
Docker both keys are present and look valid, so the test passes and the
caller uses zeros as though they were a measurement.

To decide:
  - what the POD should tell a caller to test instead (the Go zero time is
    the only reliable marker found so far, and that is engine-specific)
  - whether stats should warn or refuse on a non-running container
  - whether the streaming-hazard documentation needs a Docker-specific line

Related: #45 (the Podman side of the same endpoint).
